{"getGroupIdsWithViewPermission refactor unconditionally sets UserModel.GROUPS on the session, removing the prior canView() guard, and it is unclear whether downstream user search APIs expect raw group IDs or the previously stored resource names. Additionally, the canManage() check was silently dropped, potentially widening or narrowing access.": 0.6019928455352783, "AdminPermissionsSchema.resolveGroup() returns the raw group ID, but GroupPermissions creates and looks up resources using a 'group.resource.<id>' prefix, causing a name mismatch when finding or creating resources. This inconsistency means permission lookups will fail to locate existing group resources created by the legacy code path.": 0.6586726903915405, "Legacy GroupPermissions stores resources with type 'Group' and extracts group IDs by stripping the 'group.resource.' prefix from resource names, while GroupPermissionsV2 queries by type 'Groups' (AdminPermissionsSchema.GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE) and collects resource database IDs directly. This type and ID mismatch means V2 will return empty results for resources created by V1 code paths.": 0.656408429145813, "UserPermissionsV2.hasPermission calls AdminPermissionsSchema.SCHEMA.getResourceTypeResource which may return null, then immediately calls policyStore.findByResource(server, resource) without checking whether resource is null, risking a NullPointerException.": 0.5597949028015137, "GroupPermissionEvaluator interface has been heavily extended/modified with renamed methods (e.g. getGroupsWithViewPermission -> getGroupIdsWithViewPermission) and many new methods, creating high churn where all implementations and call sites must be updated consistently or risk compile failures or behavioral regressions.": 0.13303522765636444, "GroupPermissionsV2 throws UnsupportedOperationException for many APIs that in the legacy implementation created/returned per-group Resource or Policy objects, indicating a non-backward-compatible replacement rather than transparent migration.": 0.5551255345344543, "GroupResourceTypeEvaluationTest exercises the new GROUPS_RESOURCE_TYPE code paths but does not assert resource naming conventions or test scenarios where resources were created by the legacy GroupPermissions initializer (type 'Group', prefixed names), leaving the V1-to-V2 interoperability gap untested.": 0.36577433347702026}